# tools/qemu/local.env.example: copy to tools/qemu/local.env (git-ignored)
# and set your own paths. The launch scripts (run-*.sh) source it. They run
# under a POSIX shell on the PC (Git Bash or MSYS2 on Windows); paths QEMU
# itself reads are in the host's own form.
#
# QEMU      the qemu-system-ppc binary (a path the shell can run)
# BBOOT     the bboot boot loader QEMU loads as -kernel (AmigaOne, Pegasos II)
# MACHINES  the folder holding one folder per machine, each with its
#           kickstart zip(s) and disk images, as the scripts name them:
#             base_a1_backup\      AmigaOne: kickstart.zip, hd0.qcow2,
#                                  copies such as hd0-vnet.qcow2
#             mcpd-peg2-upd3\      Pegasos II: kickstart.zip, hd0.qcow2, ...
#             backup-sam460-upd3\  Sam460ex: hd0.qcow2, ...
#           (the disk images are copies of your own AmigaOS 4.1 FE
#           installation; see the README for which copy each script uses)
# SHARE     the PC folder the guests see as SHARED: (QEMU's 9p share)
# QEMU_OVERRIDE=<path> in the environment picks another build for one run.
QEMU="${QEMU_OVERRIDE:-/c/Program Files/qemu/qemu-system-ppc.exe}"
BBOOT='C:\path\to\bboot'
MACHINES='C:\path\to\QEMU_Machines'
SHARE='C:\path\to\shared'
